            {
              "id": "b244bf43-b30e-3699-813f-252fc32d0bf3",
              "name": "'Manage_tasks' function lacks rate-limiting decorator, risking resource starvation from high traffic volume.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `manage_tasks` function in the provided Python code is vulnerable to a Denial of Service (DoS) attack due to the lack of rate limiting. Without rate limiting, an attacker can send a large number of requests in a short period of time, potentially causing resource starvation and making the service unavailable to other users.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should implement rate limiting on this function. Rate limiting controls how many requests a user (or a group of users) can send to the function within a certain period of time. This can prevent an attacker from overwhelming the service with a flood of requests.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nYou can use Django's `ratelimit` decorator to add rate limiting to the `manage_tasks` function. Here is an example of how to apply it:\n\n```python\nfrom django_ratelimit.decorators import ratelimit\n\n@ratelimit(key='ip', rate='10/m')  # Adjust the rate as needed\ndef manage_tasks(request, project_id):\n    # The rest of your code goes here\n```\n\nIn this example, the `ratelimit` decorator limits the number of requests to 10 per minute per IP address. You can adjust the rate as needed based on your application's requirements.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Django\n- django_ratelimit (for the rate limiting fix)\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-770: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
