            {
              "id": "52a07bbd-d255-3152-8797-0c7646f1184b",
              "name": "'Manage_projects' function lacks rate-limiting decorator, potentially causing resource starvation due to high traffic.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `manage_projects` function in the provided Python code is vulnerable to a Denial of Service (DoS) attack due to the lack of rate limiting. Without rate limiting, an attacker can send a large number of requests to this function, potentially causing resource starvation and making the service unavailable to other users.\n\nRate limiting is a technique for limiting network traffic. It sets a limit on how many requests a client can make to the server in a given amount of time. When this limit is exceeded, the server will start dropping requests, which helps to prevent resource starvation and keep the service available.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should implement rate limiting on the `manage_projects` function. This can be done using a rate limiting decorator, which will limit the number of requests that a client can make to this function in a given amount of time.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how you can implement rate limiting in Django using the `django_ratelimit` library:\n\n```python\nfrom django_ratelimit.decorators import ratelimit\n\n@ratelimit(key='ip', rate='10/m')  # Limit to 10 requests per minute per IP\ndef manage_projects(request):\n    # ... existing code ...\n```\n\nIn this example, the `ratelimit` decorator is used to limit the number of requests to 10 per minute per IP address. If this limit is exceeded, the server will start dropping requests.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to be using the following libraries:\n\n- Django\n- django_ratelimit (for the rate limiting fix)\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-770: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html)"
