            {
              "id": "b755378a-e958-333d-bcc3-b1e49f332645",
              "name": "\"Shell Process Initiation: Potential Injection Vulnerability Detected\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Shell Process Initiation: Potential Injection Vulnerability Detected\" is a security vulnerability that can occur in Python programming language when the `os.system()` function is used. This function executes a shell command from a Python script. If the command string includes unsanitized user input, it can lead to command injection attacks. In such attacks, the attacker can execute arbitrary commands on the host system with the privileges of the user running the Python script.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `os.system()` whenever possible. If you need to run a shell command, use the `subprocess` module instead, which can accept a list of arguments and does not use the shell as an interpreter, thus reducing the risk of shell injection.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `os.system()`, use `subprocess.run()`. Here is an example:\n\n```python\nimport subprocess\n\n# BAD\n# os.system(\"echo \" + user_input)\n\n# GOOD\nsubprocess.run([\"echo\", user_input])\n```\n\nIn the above example, `user_input` is a variable that contains user-provided data. The `subprocess.run()` function takes a list of arguments, which are passed directly to the program without being interpreted by the shell.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `subprocess` module, which is included in the Python Standard Library and does not need to be installed separately.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command ('OS Command Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
