            {
              "id": "1e74bb52-d7b5-3e89-933d-cdcd3ffea59c",
              "name": "'Manage_groups' function lacks rate-limiting, risking resource exhaustion from high traffic.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `manage_groups` function in the provided Python code is vulnerable to resource exhaustion due to lack of rate-limiting. This means that if the function is hit with a high volume of requests in a short period of time, it could consume all available system resources, leading to a denial of service (DoS) condition. This is a security risk as it can be exploited by an attacker to make the application unavailable to legitimate users.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should implement rate-limiting on the `manage_groups` function. Rate limiting is a technique for limiting network traffic. It sets a limit on how many requests a client can make to the server in a given amount of time.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nYou can use Django Ratelimit, a Django middleware for rate limiting, to fix this vulnerability. Here is how you can do it:\n\nFirst, install Django Ratelimit using pip:\n\n```bash\npip install django_ratelimit\n```\n\nThen, add `'ratelimit.middleware.RatelimitMiddleware'` to your `MIDDLEWARE` setting:\n\n```python\nMIDDLEWARE = [\n    # ...\n    'ratelimit.middleware.RatelimitMiddleware',\n    # ...\n]\n```\n\nFinally, apply the rate limit to the `manage_groups` function:\n\n```python\nfrom ratelimit.decorators import ratelimit\n\n@ratelimit(key='ip', rate='10/m')  # Adjust the rate as needed\ndef manage_groups(request):\n    # ...\n```\n\nThis will limit the number of requests to 10 per minute from a single IP address.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- Django\n- Django Ratelimit\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-770: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html)"
