            {
              "id": "7082eded-df77-335f-ac9c-0c9496127469",
              "name": "'Upload' function lacks rate-limiting decorator, potentially causing resource starvation due to high traffic volume.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe 'Upload' function in the provided Python code lacks a rate-limiting decorator. This means that there is no limit to the number of requests that can be made to this function in a given time period. This can potentially lead to resource starvation if the function receives a high volume of traffic, as each request consumes server resources. This is a type of Denial of Service (DoS) attack.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should implement rate limiting on the 'Upload' function. This can be done using a decorator that limits the number of requests that can be made to the function in a given time period. This will prevent resource starvation by ensuring that the server is not overwhelmed by a high volume of requests.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nYou can use Django Ratelimit library to add rate limiting to your function. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```python\nfrom django_ratelimit.decorators import ratelimit\n\n@ratelimit(key='ip', rate='10/m')  # Adjust the rate as needed\ndef upload(request, project_id):\n    # rest of the code\n```\n\nIn the above code, the `@ratelimit` decorator is used to limit the number of requests that can be made to the 'Upload' function from a single IP address to 10 per minute. You can adjust the rate as needed.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- Django\n- Django Ratelimit\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-770: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html)"
              },
