            {
              "id": "760a3b68-0199-3e22-8844-50074b9fc455",
              "name": "\"SQL Injection Vulnerability Detected in Data Passed to execute() Function\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet provided is vulnerable to SQL Injection attacks. This is due to the use of string formatting (`%s`) to include user-provided data directly into the SQL query. An attacker could manipulate the `name` or `upload_path` parameters to alter the SQL query, potentially leading to unauthorized data access, data corruption, or data loss.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use parameterized queries or prepared statements, which can ensure that user-provided data is always treated as literal values, not part of the SQL command. This can effectively prevent SQL Injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the code:\n\n```python\nname = request.POST.get('name', False)\nupload_path = store_uploaded_file(name, request.FILES['file'])\n\n#A1 - Injection (SQLi)\ncurs = connection.cursor()\nquery = \"insert into taskManager_file ('name','path','project_id') values (%s,%s,%s)\"\ncurs.execute(query, (name, upload_path, project_id))\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following Python libraries:\n\n- Django (for `request.POST.get` and `connection.cursor`)\n- A custom function or library to handle `store_uploaded_file`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
