            {
              "id": "ff98e7de-2859-38b7-a9c3-0f33c708aa09",
              "name": "\"Download Function Lacks Rate-Limiting Decorator Vulnerability\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Download Function Lacks Rate-Limiting Decorator Vulnerability\" in Python refers to a situation where a function that handles file downloads does not have a mechanism to limit the rate at which requests can be made. This can lead to a Denial of Service (DoS) attack, where an attacker can overwhelm the server by making a large number of download requests in a short period of time.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should implement a rate-limiting mechanism on the download function. This can be done using decorators in Python. A decorator is a function that takes another function and extends the behavior of the latter function without explicitly modifying it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how you can implement rate limiting using a decorator in Django:\n\n```python\nfrom django_ratelimit.decorators import ratelimit\n\n@ratelimit(key='ip', rate='10/m')  # limit to 10 requests per minute per IP\ndef download(request, file_id):\n    file = File.objects.get(pk=file_id)\n    abspath = open(\n        os.path.dirname(\n            os.path.realpath(__file__)) +\n        file.path,\n        'rb')\n    response = HttpResponse(content=abspath.read())\n    response['Content-Type'] = mimetypes.guess_type(file.path)[0]\n    response['Content-Disposition'] = 'attachment; filename=%s' % file.name\n    return response\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- Django\n- django_ratelimit\n- os\n- mimetypes\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-770: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
