            {
              "id": "6be4006a-344e-3898-98f8-f0bc5b8a6ccd",
              "name": "Unclosed File Object Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnclosed File Object Vulnerability in Python occurs when a file object is opened but not properly closed. This can lead to resource leaks, as the file descriptor remains open, and can potentially be exploited for denial-of-service attacks. It can also lead to data corruption if the program crashes before the data is fully written to the disk.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that file objects are properly closed after use. This can be done by using the `close()` method of the file object. However, a better practice is to use the `with` statement, which automatically closes the file when the block of code is exited, even if an exception is raised within the block.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is the recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```python\nimport os\n\nwith open(\n    os.path.dirname(\n        os.path.realpath(__file__)) +\n    file.path,\n    'rb'\n) as abspath:\n    # use abspath here\n    pass\n```\n\nIn this code, the `with` statement is used to ensure that the file object `abspath` is properly closed after use.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `os` library, which is a standard library in Python and does not need to be installed separately.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-775: Missing Release of File Descriptor or Handle after Effective Lifetime](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/775.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
