            {
              "id": "d88c13c0-cd0f-3ca6-b6ea-c599315739d3",
              "name": "Potential XSS Vulnerability through Direct Data Rendering via HttpResponse in Django",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn this case, the vulnerability arises from directly rendering data through HttpResponse in Django. The data read from `abspath` is directly sent to the client without any sanitization or encoding, which can lead to potential XSS attacks if the data contains any malicious scripts.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always escape untrusted data based on the HTML context (body, attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL) that the data will be placed into. Django provides a range of tools and functions to escape HTML and JavaScript, which can be used to prevent XSS attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of directly sending the data to the client, you should use Django's built-in escaping functions to sanitize the data. Here is an example of how you can do this:\n\n```python\nfrom django.utils.html import escape\nresponse = HttpResponse(content=escape(abspath.read()))\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Django\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
