            {
              "id": "24cbd613-2cb0-37ab-b197-e670c3e9b187",
              "name": "\"task_create\" function lacks rate-limiting, risking resource exhaustion with high traffic.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `task_create` function in the provided Python code is vulnerable to a Denial of Service (DoS) attack due to the lack of rate-limiting. An attacker can send a large number of POST requests to the server, causing the server to exhaust its resources while trying to process these requests. This can lead to service disruption, slow response times, and even server crashes.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should implement rate-limiting on the `task_create` function. Rate-limiting is a technique for limiting network traffic. It sets a limit on how many requests a client can make to the server in a certain amount of time.\n\n## Code Fix Recommendation\n\nYou can use Django's `ratelimit` decorator to easily add rate limiting to your view. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```python\nfrom django_ratelimit.decorators import ratelimit\n\n@ratelimit(key='ip', rate='10/m')  # Adjust the rate as needed\ndef task_create(request, project_id):\n    # ... rest of your code\n```\n\nIn this example, the rate '10/m' means that the client can make 10 requests per minute. If a client exceeds this limit, the decorator will add a 'limited' attribute to the request, and you can decide how to respond. For example, you can return a 429 'Too Many Requests' response.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Django\n- django-ratelimit (for the rate limiting solution)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Denial of Service](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Denial_of_Service)\n- [CWE-770: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-770",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-770"
