            {
              "id": "24e10189-fbc8-3357-8415-d2082fcc0132",
              "name": "\"Task_edit function lacks rate-limiting decorator vulnerability\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `task_edit` function in the provided Python code is vulnerable to a type of security issue known as \"rate-limiting\" or \"brute-force\" attacks. This is because it lacks a rate-limiting decorator, which is a mechanism that limits the rate at which a certain part of the system can be accessed. Without this, an attacker could potentially send a large number of requests in a short period of time, which could lead to a denial of service (DoS) attack, or they could attempt to guess the correct parameters for `project_id` and `task_id` through brute force.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should implement a rate-limiting mechanism. This can be done using decorators in Python. A decorator is a special kind of function that either modifies the behavior of other functions or classes or adds new functionality. In this case, you would use a decorator to add rate-limiting functionality to the `task_edit` function.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how you could add a rate-limiting decorator to the `task_edit` function using the Django Ratelimit library:\n\n```python\nfrom django_ratelimit.decorators import ratelimit\n\n@ratelimit(key='ip', rate='10/m')  # Limit to 10 requests per minute per IP\ndef task_edit(request, project_id, task_id):\n    # ... rest of the function ...\n```\n\nIn this example, the `@ratelimit` decorator is used to limit the number of requests that can be made to the `task_edit` function to 10 per minute for each IP address.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- Django\n- Django Ratelimit (for the rate-limiting decorator)\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-770: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
