            {
              "id": "722bf57d-df92-3ffb-8159-bd271ad5437a",
              "name": "'Missing Rate-Limiting Decorator in 'task_complete' Function'",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability here is the absence of a rate-limiting mechanism in the `task_complete` function. This function is used to mark a task as completed in a project. Without rate limiting, an attacker can potentially send a large number of requests to this function in a short period of time, causing a Denial of Service (DoS) attack or brute force attack. This can lead to server overload and potential crashes, or unauthorized access to sensitive data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should implement a rate-limiting mechanism. This can be done using decorators in Python. A decorator is a design pattern in Python that allows a user to add new functionality to an existing object without modifying its structure. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how you can implement rate limiting using a decorator:\n\n```python\nfrom django.core.exceptions import PermissionDenied\nfrom django_ratelimit.decorators import ratelimit\n\n@ratelimit(key='ip', rate='10/m', block=True)\ndef task_complete(request, project_id, task_id):\n    proj = Project.objects.get(pk=project_id)\n    task = Task.objects.get(pk=task_id)\n    if proj is not None:\n        if task is not None and task.project == proj:\n            task.completed = not task.completed\n            task.save()\n\n    return redirect('/taskManager/' + project_id)\n```\n\nIn this example, the `@ratelimit` decorator is used to limit the number of requests to 10 per minute per IP address. If the limit is exceeded, the request is blocked and a `PermissionDenied` exception is raised.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Django\n- django-ratelimit\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-770: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html)"
