            {
              "id": "26fdae2f-94e2-39a0-9261-4d79baee1b8e",
              "name": "\"Task_delete function lacks rate-limiting decorator, risking resource exhaustion with high traffic.\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `task_delete` function in the provided Python code is vulnerable to a Denial of Service (DoS) attack due to the lack of rate limiting. Without rate limiting, an attacker can send a large number of requests to this endpoint, potentially exhausting system resources and causing service disruption.\n\nRate limiting is a technique for limiting network traffic. It sets a limit on how many requests a client can make to a server in a given amount of time. Without rate limiting, your application is vulnerable to attacks where an attacker can send a large number of requests, potentially causing your application to exhaust its resources and crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should implement rate limiting on this endpoint. This can be done using Django's built-in rate limiting or using a third-party library such as Django Ratelimit.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how you can implement rate limiting using Django Ratelimit:\n\n```python\nfrom django_ratelimit.decorators import ratelimit\n\n@ratelimit(key='ip', rate='10/m')  # Adjust the rate as needed\ndef task_delete(request, project_id, task_id):\n    proj = Project.objects.get(pk=project_id)\n    task = Task.objects.get(pk=task_id)\n    if proj is not None:\n        if task is not None and task.project == proj:\n            task.delete()\n\n    return redirect('/taskManager/' + project_id + '/')\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Django\n- Django Ratelimit\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Denial of Service](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Denial_of_Service)\n- [CWE-770: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html)"
