            {
              "id": "482409c2-3748-388e-b6dc-ba4cc1ab884d",
              "name": "Use ITEM.user_id instead of ITEM.user.id to avoid additional query execution.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn Python, when you access a related object through a foreign key, Django has to make a database query to fetch the related object. This is known as a database join operation. If you access a related object's attribute (like `ITEM.user.id`), Django makes an additional database query to fetch the related object, even if you only need the ID of the related object. This can lead to performance issues due to unnecessary database queries.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo avoid this, you can use the `ITEM.user_id` attribute instead. This attribute is created automatically by Django for every `ForeignKey` field. The `user_id` attribute contains the ID of the related object, and accessing it does not require a database query. This can significantly improve the performance of your code.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```python\nproject.users_assigned = [request.user.id]\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```python\nproject.users_assigned = [request.user_id]\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Django\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)\n\nPlease note that the links are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
