              "id": "1dbd4dd2-5efa-3f5d-a3e5-cb9eb76794ef",
              "name": "'project_edit' function lacks rate-limiting decorator, posing a potential resource exhaustion risk.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `project_edit` function in the provided Python code is vulnerable to a potential resource exhaustion attack because it lacks a rate-limiting mechanism. This means that an attacker could potentially send a large number of requests to this function in a short period of time, causing the server to consume excessive resources and potentially leading to a Denial of Service (DoS) condition.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, a rate-limiting decorator should be added to the function. This will limit the number of requests that can be made to the function within a certain time period, preventing an attacker from overwhelming the server with requests.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how you could add a rate-limiting decorator to the `project_edit` function using Django's `ratelimit` decorator:\n\n```python\nfrom django_ratelimit.decorators import ratelimit\n\n@ratelimit(key='ip', rate='10/m')  # Adjust rate as needed\ndef project_edit(request, project_id):\n    # ... rest of the function ...\n```\n\nIn this example, the `ratelimit` decorator is used to limit the number of requests that can be made to the function from a single IP address to 10 per minute. You can adjust the rate as needed for your specific use case.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to depend on the following libraries:\n\n- Django (for the `Project` model, `RequestContext`, `redirect`, and `render_to_response` functions)\n- django-ratelimit (for the `ratelimit` decorator in the recommended fix)\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-770: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html)"
