            {
              "id": "c471100c-2314-3c2c-937a-0ba0ae307f37",
              "name": "'project_create' function lacks rate-limiting decorator vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `project_create` function in the provided Python code is vulnerable to a Denial of Service (DoS) attack because it lacks a rate-limiting mechanism. This means that an attacker can send a large number of requests to this function in a short period of time, potentially causing the server to become overwhelmed and unresponsive to other users.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should implement a rate-limiting mechanism on the `project_create` function. This can be done using Django's built-in rate limiting features or by using a third-party library such as Django Ratelimit.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix using Django Ratelimit:\n\n```python\nfrom django_ratelimit.decorators import ratelimit\n\n@ratelimit(key='ip', rate='10/m')  # Adjust rate as needed\ndef project_create(request):\n    # Existing code...\n```\n\nThis will limit the number of requests to 10 per minute per IP address. Adjust the rate as needed for your specific use case.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Django\n- Django Ratelimit (for the fix)\n- Python's built-in `datetime` module\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-770: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active at the time of writing."
