            {
              "id": "27d5cd46-f469-3df6-97f4-9bd10a33c57b",
              "name": "\"Register Function Lacks Rate-Limiting Decorator Vulnerability\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Register Function Lacks Rate-Limiting Decorator Vulnerability\" in Python refers to the absence of a mechanism to limit the rate of requests that can be made to the `register` function. This can lead to a potential Denial of Service (DoS) attack, where an attacker can flood the system with registration requests, causing the system to slow down or even crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should implement a rate-limiting mechanism. This can be done using Django's built-in rate limiting or using third-party libraries such as Django Ratelimit. Rate limiting will ensure that a user can only make a certain number of requests within a specified time period.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix using Django Ratelimit library:\n\n```python\nfrom django_ratelimit.decorators import ratelimit\n\n@ratelimit(key='ip', rate='10/m')  # limit to 10 requests per minute per IP\ndef register(request):\n    # ... existing code ...\n    was_limited = getattr(request, 'limited', False)\n    if was_limited:\n        # If rate limit was exceeded, return an error response\n        return HttpResponse('Too many requests', status=429)\n    # ... existing code ...\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Django\n- Django Ratelimit (for the fix)\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-770: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html)"
