              "id": "8f1b728f-0a34-3560-8f0c-b0d4cb1631bc",
              "name": "'logout_view' function lacks rate-limiting decorator, potentially causing resource starvation due to high traffic.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `logout_view` function in the provided Python code lacks a rate-limiting decorator. This means that there is no limit to the number of requests that can be made to this function in a given time period. This can potentially lead to resource starvation if the function is hit with a high volume of traffic, as each request consumes server resources. This is a type of Denial of Service (DoS) attack.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should implement rate limiting on this function. This can be done using a decorator that limits the number of requests that can be made to this function within a certain time period. This will prevent an attacker from overwhelming the server with requests to this function.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how you can implement rate limiting using the Django Ratelimit library:\n\n```python\nfrom django_ratelimit.decorators import ratelimit\n\n@ratelimit(key='ip', rate='10/m')\ndef logout_view(request):\n    was_limited = getattr(request, 'limited', False)\n    if was_limited:\n        # You can return a custom response here\n        return HttpResponse('Too many requests')\n    logout(request)\n    return redirect(request.GET.get('redirect', '/taskManager/'))\n```\n\nIn this example, the `@ratelimit` decorator limits the number of requests to 10 per minute per IP address. If the limit is exceeded, a custom response is returned.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Django\n- Django Ratelimit\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-770: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html)"
