            {
              "id": "4ec28f18-dc0d-3a74-9e97-9031c1228e27",
              "name": "'project_delete' function lacks rate-limiting decorator, potentially causing resource starvation due to high traffic.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `project_delete` function in the provided Python code is vulnerable to a potential Denial of Service (DoS) attack due to the lack of rate limiting. Without rate limiting, an attacker can send a large number of requests to this endpoint, causing resource starvation and potentially leading to a DoS condition where legitimate users are unable to access the service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should implement rate limiting on this function. Rate limiting controls the number of requests a client can make to a server in a specific period of time. This can prevent an attacker from overwhelming the server with a high volume of requests.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nYou can use Django's `ratelimit` decorator to add rate limiting to the `project_delete` function. Here is an example of how to apply it:\n\n```python\nfrom django_ratelimit.decorators import ratelimit\n\n@ratelimit(key='ip', rate='10/m')  # Adjust the rate as needed\ndef project_delete(request, project_id):\n    # IDOR\n    project = Project.objects.get(pk=project_id)\n    project.delete()\n    return redirect('/taskManager/dashboard')\n```\n\nIn this example, the `ratelimit` decorator limits the number of requests to 10 per minute from a single IP address.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Django\n- django-ratelimit\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-770: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
