            {
              "id": "ccd2742b-ee4f-3b2d-ad7f-5fcdc0283d51",
              "name": "\"Index Function Lacks Rate-Limiting Decorator Vulnerability\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Index Function Lacks Rate-Limiting Decorator Vulnerability\" in Python refers to the absence of a rate-limiting mechanism on a function that handles requests. This can lead to Denial of Service (DoS) attacks, where an attacker sends a large number of requests in a short period of time, causing the server to become overwhelmed and unresponsive.\n\nIn the provided code, the `index` function handles incoming requests but does not have a rate-limiting mechanism in place. This makes it susceptible to DoS attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, a rate-limiting decorator can be added to the function. This will limit the number of requests that can be made to the function within a certain time frame.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how to add a rate-limiting decorator using Django Ratelimit library:\n\n```python\nfrom django_ratelimit.decorators import ratelimit\n\n@ratelimit(key='ip', rate='10/m')  # limit to 10 requests per minute per IP\ndef index(request):\n    # existing code here\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Django\n- Django Ratelimit (for the fix)\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-770: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing."
