              "id": "d2dfd685-66c0-39d9-ae6d-df44427f5b54",
              "name": "'User' password set without validation in Django",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn Django, the `set_password()` method is used to set a user's password. This method automatically handles the hashing and salting of the password, ensuring that the password is securely stored. However, if the `set_password()` method is used without any form of validation, it can lead to security vulnerabilities. For example, a user might be able to set a weak password, such as \"password\" or \"123456\", which can be easily guessed or cracked by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to enforce password complexity rules. This can be done by using Django's built-in password validation, which can be configured to require a minimum length, prohibit common passwords, and require a mix of uppercase and lowercase letters, digits, and special characters.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to use Django's built-in password validation:\n\n```python\nfrom django.contrib.auth.password_validation import validate_password\nfrom django.core.exceptions import ValidationError\n\ntry:\n    validate_password(user.password)\nexcept ValidationError as e:\n    # Handle the validation error\n    print(e)\nelse:\n    user.set_password(user.password)\n```\n\nIn this example, the `validate_password()` function is used to validate the password before it is set. If the password does not meet the validation requirements, a `ValidationError` is raised.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Django\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Password Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Password Policy](https://owasp.org/www-project-web-security-testing-guide/latest/4-Web_Application_Security_Testing/03-Identity_Management_Testing/02-Test_User_Registration_Process.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-521: Weak Password Requirements](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/521.html)"
              },
