            {
              "id": "ced91a5d-26fc-3a52-ad48-e1c5ffd12200",
              "name": "Use ITEM.user_id instead of ITEM.user.id to avoid additional query execution.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn Python, when dealing with object-relational mapping (ORM) frameworks like Django, SQLAlchemy, etc., accessing related objects through dot notation (like `ITEM.user.id`) can lead to additional database queries, which can degrade performance and potentially expose your application to SQL injection attacks if not handled properly. This is because the ORM needs to fetch the related `user` object from the database before it can access its `id` attribute.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo avoid this, you can use the `ITEM.user_id` notation instead. This directly accesses the foreign key field on the `ITEM` object, which does not require an additional database query. This can improve performance and reduce the risk of SQL injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```python\nusers_assigned=request.user.id\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```python\nusers_assigned=request.user_id\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example seems to be using Django, so the required library is Django.\n\n```bash\npip install Django\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8d18b1f7-dd43-3b96-82ee-f107835fe7b6",
              "name": "Use ITEM.user_id instead of ITEM.user.id to avoid additional query execution.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn Python, when you access a related field, Django has to make a database query to fetch the related object. This is known as a \"database hit\". If you're not careful, you can end up making a lot of unnecessary database hits, which can slow down your application.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `request.user.id` is used to fetch the user's ID. This is a direct access to the user's ID, which doesn't require an additional database hit. However, if you were to use `request.user.id` instead, Django would have to make an additional database hit to fetch the user object before it can access the ID. This is because `request.user` is a related field, and accessing it requires a database hit.\n\nThis is not a security vulnerability per se, but it can lead to performance issues if not handled properly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo avoid unnecessary database hits, you should always access related fields directly whenever possible. In this case, you should use `request.user.id` instead of `request.user.id`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is the corrected code:\n\n```python\nusers_assigned=request.user.id).order_by('title')\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example seems to be using Django, a Python web framework. Therefore, the Django library is required for the code to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [CWE-400: Uncontrolled Resource Consumption](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not be available in the future."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-400",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-400"
                ]
              }
