                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `note_create` function in the provided Python code is vulnerable to a potential resource exhaustion attack because it lacks a rate-limiting mechanism. This means that an attacker could potentially send a large number of requests to this function in a short period of time, causing the server to consume excessive resources and potentially leading to a Denial of Service (DoS) condition.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to implement a rate-limiting mechanism on the `note_create` function. This can be achieved by using a decorator that limits the number of requests that a client can make to this function within a certain time period.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how you could implement rate limiting using Django's `ratelimit` decorator:\n\n```python\nfrom django_ratelimit.decorators import ratelimit\n\n@ratelimit(key='ip', rate='10/m')  # Limit to 10 requests per minute per IP\ndef note_create(request, project_id, task_id):\n    # ... existing code ...\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to depend on the following libraries:\n\n- Django (for handling HTTP requests and responses, ORM, etc.)\n- django_ratelimit (for rate limiting)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Denial of Service](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Denial_of_Service)\n- [CWE-770: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html)"
