              "id": "20e909a6-5887-399c-9c9c-8feb2578811d",
              "name": "'note_delete' function lacks rate-limiting decorator, potentially causing resource starvation due to high traffic.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `note_delete` function in the provided Python code is vulnerable to a Denial of Service (DoS) attack due to the lack of rate limiting. Without rate limiting, an attacker can send a large number of requests to this function, potentially causing resource starvation and making the service unavailable to other users.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should implement rate limiting on this function. Rate limiting controls how many requests a user can send to a function within a certain time period. This can prevent an attacker from sending a large number of requests in a short amount of time.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nYou can use Django's `ratelimit` decorator to add rate limiting to the `note_delete` function. Here is an example of how to do this:\n\n```python\nfrom django_ratelimit.decorators import ratelimit\n\n@ratelimit(key='ip', rate='10/m')  # Limit to 10 requests per minute per IP\ndef note_delete(request, project_id, task_id, note_id):\n    proj = Project.objects.get(pk=project_id)\n    task = Task.objects.get(pk=task_id)\n    note = Notes.objects.get(pk=note_id)\n    if proj is not None:\n        if task is not None and task.project == proj:\n            if note is not None and note.task == task:\n                note.delete()\n\n    return redirect('/taskManager/' + project_id + '/' + task_id)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Django\n- django-ratelimit\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Denial of Service](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Denial_of_Service)\n- [CWE-770: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html)"
