              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe function `dashboard` in the provided Python code lacks a rate-limiting decorator. This means that there is no mechanism in place to limit the number of requests that an individual user can make to this function within a certain time period. This can lead to resource starvation, where the server's resources are consumed by handling an excessive number of requests, potentially leading to a denial of service (DoS) condition.\n\nRate limiting is a technique for preventing network attacks, such as brute force or DDoS attacks, by limiting the number of requests a user can make to a server within a certain time period.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should implement rate limiting on the `dashboard` function. This can be done using a decorator that limits the number of requests a user can make within a certain time period.\n\nIn Python, you can use the Django Ratelimit library to easily add rate limiting to your views. The `ratelimit(key, rate, method, block=False)` decorator can be used to limit the rate of requests to a view.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can add rate limiting to the `dashboard` function using the Django Ratelimit library:\n\n```python\nfrom django_ratelimit.decorators import ratelimit\n\n@ratelimit(key='ip', rate='10/m', block=True)\ndef dashboard(request):\n    sorted_projects = Project.objects.filter(\n        users_assigned=request.user.id).order_by('title')\n    sorted_tasks = Task.objects.filter(\n        users_assigned=request.user.id).order_by('title')\n    return render(request,\n                  'taskManager/dashboard.html',\n                  {'project_list': sorted_projects,\n                   'user': request.user,\n                   'task_list': sorted_tasks})\n```\n\nIn this example, the `ratelimit` decorator is used to limit the number of requests to 10 per minute per IP address. If a user exceeds this limit, their requests will be blocked.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- Django\n- Django Ratelimit\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-770: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html)"
