            {
              "id": "4cd330f5-f5b3-3639-9149-a0f505b6e03c",
              "name": "Use ITEM.user_id instead of ITEM.user.id to avoid additional query execution.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn Python, when you access a related field, Django has to make a database query to fetch the related object. This is known as a \"database hit\". If you're not careful, you can end up making a lot of unnecessary database hits, which can slow down your application.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `request.user.id` is used to fetch the user's ID. This is a direct access to the user's ID, which doesn't require an additional database hit. However, if you were to use `request.user.id` instead, Django would have to make an additional database hit to fetch the user object before it can access the ID. This is because `request.user` is a related field, and accessing it requires a database hit.\n\nThis is not a security vulnerability per se, but it can lead to performance issues if not handled properly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo avoid unnecessary database hits, you should always access related fields directly whenever possible. In this case, you should use `request.user.id` instead of `request.user.id`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is the corrected code:\n\n```python\nusers_assigned=request.user.id).order_by('title')\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example seems to be using Django, a Python web framework. Therefore, the Django library is required for the code to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [CWE-400: Uncontrolled Resource Consumption](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not be available in the future."
