            {
              "id": "9edd99c0-49f8-3134-bb01-51f736aeaa4b",
              "name": "\"Task_list function lacks rate-limiting decorator, risking resource starvation due to high traffic.\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `task_list` function in the provided Python code is vulnerable to a Denial of Service (DoS) attack due to the lack of rate limiting. Without rate limiting, an attacker can send a large number of requests to this function, potentially causing resource starvation and making the service unavailable to other users.\n\nRate limiting is a technique for limiting network traffic. It sets a limit on how many requests a client can make to the server in a given amount of time. Without rate limiting, your application can be vulnerable to DoS attacks, as an attacker can overwhelm your server with a flood of requests.\n\n## Mitigation\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should implement rate limiting on this function. Django provides a number of ways to do this, including using Django Ratelimit, a third-party library that provides a decorator you can use to add rate limiting to your views.\n\n## Code Fix\n\nHere is how you could modify the `task_list` function to include rate limiting:\n\n```python\nfrom django_ratelimit.decorators import ratelimit\n\n@ratelimit(key='ip', rate='10/m')  # Adjust rate as needed\ndef task_list(request):\n    my_task_list = Task.objects.filter(users_assigned=request.user.id)\n    return render(request, 'taskManager/task_list.html',\n                  {'task_list': my_task_list, 'user': request.user})\n```\n\n## Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- Django\n- Django Ratelimit\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-770: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html)"
