            {
              "id": "63453d5c-50e1-351a-9435-bb9c8b083e3b",
              "name": "\"Rate-Limiting Decorator Missing in 'Tutorials' Function\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Rate-Limiting Decorator Missing in 'Tutorials' Function\" vulnerability in Python refers to the absence of a rate-limiting mechanism in a function that handles HTTP requests. This can potentially lead to Denial of Service (DoS) attacks, where an attacker sends a large number of requests to the function, causing the server to become overwhelmed and unresponsive.\n\nIn the provided code, the `tutorials` function handles HTTP requests but does not implement any form of rate limiting. This means that if an attacker sends a large number of requests to this function, it could potentially overwhelm the server.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, a rate-limiting mechanism should be implemented. This can be done using decorators provided by Django's `ratelimit` library. The `ratelimit` decorator can be used to specify how many requests a client can make in a certain time period.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```python\nfrom django.views.decorators.ratelimit import ratelimit\n\n@ratelimit(key='ip', rate='10/m')\ndef tutorials(request):\n    return render(request,\n                  'taskManager/tutorials.html',\n                  {'user': request.user})\n```\n\nIn this code, the `ratelimit` decorator is used to limit the number of requests to 10 per minute per IP address.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- Django\n- django-ratelimit\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-770: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
