            {
              "id": "6215d2cb-b347-389e-a0bb-c17f6c68ef3f",
              "name": "\"Profile\" function lacks rate-limiting decorator, risking resource starvation due to high traffic volume.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Profile\" function in the provided Python code lacks a rate-limiting decorator. This means that there is no control over how many times this function can be called within a certain period of time. This can lead to resource starvation if the function is called too frequently, such as in a Denial of Service (DoS) attack where an attacker makes a large number of requests to the function in a short period of time, consuming server resources and potentially causing the service to become unavailable for other users.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should implement rate limiting on this function. This can be done using a decorator that limits the number of times the function can be called within a certain period of time. This will prevent an attacker from being able to make a large number of requests in a short period of time.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how you can implement rate limiting using the Django Ratelimit library:\n\n```python\nfrom django_ratelimit.decorators import ratelimit\n\n@ratelimit(key='ip', rate='10/m')\ndef profile(request):\n    return render(request, 'taskManager/profile.html', {'user': request.user})\n```\n\nIn this example, the `@ratelimit` decorator is used to limit the number of times the `profile` function can be called to 10 times per minute per IP address.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- Django\n- Django Ratelimit\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-770: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html)"
